If the first parameter of a method on a class object is $super, Prototype's code for setting up the class (in 
addMethod)  inserts a reference to the superclass.  Here's the relevant code:

Class.Methods {
  addMethods: function(source) {
    var ancestor   = this.superclass && this.superclass.prototype;
    var properties = Object.keys(source);
   ...
   for (var i = 0, length = properties.length; i < length; i++) {
      var property = properties[i], value = source[property];
      if (ancestor && Object.isFunction(value) &&
          value.argumentNames().first() == "$super") {
         ....

Alan added a change in November to avoid renaming $super by marking it as an exported symbol in the 
DefaultCodingConvention.  To correctly compile Prototype code in advanced mode, we'll need to make sure 
the setup code for the class correctly does the parameter substitution.

See
http://www.prototypejs.org/assets/2008/9/29/prototype-1.6.0.3.js
http://www.prototypejs.org/api/class/create

So after looking into this some more, I've learned that arrays are always passed by reference, but that only 
means you can change attributes and individual items in an array. So for example:

var a = [1, 2, 3, 4];
var f = function(x){x = null;};
f(a);
// the value of a will remain [1, 2, 3, 4]
// but if instead:
var g = function(x){x[0] = null;};
g(a);
// the value of a is now [null, 2, 3, 4]

In the attached example, the Google Closure compiled version is akin to the function f above, which means 
that the value of the arrays should *not* be changing. In IE, if the threshold is set high enough, however, they 
do indeed change. So the original report may not be accurate, but this is a real issue.

